#DESCRIPTION
Hybrid PIC (kinetic ions / fluid electrons) parallel whistler-Alfven wave test.
Hall-only: validates the Hall term of the generalized Ohm's law in normalized
CGS units (resistivity and electron-pressure-gradient terms disabled).

Parameters are chosen so that in CODE (normalized) units the ion scales are
O(1) and the ion cyclotron motion is resolved by the timestep:
  Geometry in code units; box Lx = 6.4 d_i. With lNormSI = uNormSI = 1.0e5,
  tNorm = 1 s so TimeMax (s) equals code time; with q/(m c) = 1 for the proton,
  B = Omega_i ~ 1.  With dt = 0.02, dt*Omega_i = 0.02 << 1 so the Hall/whistler
  mode is numerically stable.  The right-hand Hall whistler at k d_i = 0.982 has
  omega/Omega_i = [k^2 d_i^2 + k d_i sqrt(k^2 d_i^2 + 4)]/2 = 1.617, i.e. period
  T = 3.89 s (the left-hand/ion-cyclotron branch would be at 0.618).

#INITFROMSWMF
F                       initFromSWMF

#GEOMETRY
-3.2                    xMin  [code units; Lx = 6.4 d_i]
 3.2                    xMax
-0.05                   yMin
 0.05                   yMax
-0.05                   zMin
 0.05                   zMax

#NCELL
64                      nCellX
1                       nCellY
1                       nCellZ

#MAXBLOCKSIZE
64                      maxBlockSizeX
1                       maxBlockSizeY
1                       maxBlockSizeZ

#PERIODICITY
T                       isPeriodicX
T                       isPeriodicY
T                       isPeriodicZ

#SOLVEEM
F                       solveEM

#HYBRIDPIC
T                       useHybridPIC

#RESISTIVITY
0.0                     etaResistivity  [m^2/s, SI magnetic diffusivity]

Grid-scale hyper-resistivity, REQUIRED for a long hybrid wave run.  The
explicit Hall advance of this cold, low-beta plasma is unstable to a
non-propagating (symmetric +-n) mode at the few-cell scale: measured on this
deck (k d_i ~ 1 seed, 24 s, 64 cells) the strongest growth is at lambda ~ 3
cells (n ~ +-19) with gamma ~ 0.65 /Omega_i, while the k d_i <= 6 physical range
is stable.  Two independent checks pin it on the Hall-term discretization: it is
independent of the particle count (20000 ppc: unchanged) and it DISAPPEARS with
the Hall term switched off (useHallTerm = F: gamma = -0.007), while more B
sub-cycling does not help (nBSubcycle = 32: gamma = +0.50).

NOTE (a real footgun, hit while writing this comment): never write a hash-name
token such as the text of a command block inside a comment line of a PARAM.in
file.  The FLEKS reader scans for a command keyword anywhere in the text,
including mid-line, so a comment mentioning, say, the HALLTERM block silently
builds a real HALLTERM command whose value is taken from the first token of the
NEXT line.  That is exactly what happened here: the previous version of this
comment contained the literal keyword of the Hall-term block followed by "F",
which set useHallTerm to the word "not" (false) and disabled the Hall term, so
the seeded mode looked unstable (21x growth in 24 s instead of flat).  The
startup echo in the run log prints every command the reader actually built
(lines beginning with "PC:") and is the quickest way to spot this.

Grid mode sets eta_h = 4*pi * etaHyperCh * dx^4 / dt, i.e. a fixed damping per
step of gamma_h = etaHyperCh * (k dx)^4 / dt_sim, so it acts only on the
badly-resolved harmonics.  With etaHyperCh = 1e-2 and dx = 0.1:

    n = 1  (k d_i = 1.0)  : (k dx)^4 = 9.3e-5 -> gamma_h = 4.6e-5  (seed
                            damping ~1e-5 of omega, i.e. unmeasurable)
    m = 6  (k d_i = 6.0)  : (k dx)^4 = 0.12   -> gamma_h = 0.060 vs
                            omega = 36, so < 1 % amplitude loss per period
    n = 19 (lambda ~ 3.4) : (k dx)^4 = 12.1   -> gamma_h = 6.0, far above the
                            instability's 0.65

Measured effect (identical runs, only etaHyperCh changed): the unstable band
growth drops from +0.65 (off) to +0.42 (1e-3) to no growth at all (1e-2), and
max|B_perp| at t = 24 s goes from 2.22 (5x the 0.0200 seed and growing) to
0.0267 - the seed's own level.  The seed mode itself is untouched: gamma =
-0.0024 versus -0.0019 without it.  etaHyperCh >= 1e-2 gives the same result
(the damping already saturates the band), so 1e-2 is the smallest value that
cleans the whole unstable band.  With the deck exactly as below the 24 s check
run gives |C(n=1)| = 0.0200 -> 0.0262 (gamma = -0.0000), |C(n=19)| = 0.0000
throughout, and max|B_perp| = 0.0200 -> 0.0267; without the block the same run
leaves |C(n=19)| = 0.085 and growing.
#HYPERRESISTIVITY
0.0                     etaHyperSI
grid                    etaHyperMode
1.0e-2                  etaHyperCh

#ELECTRONTEMPERATURE
0.0                     electronTemperature  [eV]
1.0                     electronGamma
1.0                     electronDensity0

Sub-cycle the B update. The explicit Hall advance is limited by the discrete
grid-scale whistler mode, omega_max * dt_sub < S (S ~ 2.83 for RK4). Because the
compact curl operators (curl_center_to_node / curl_node_to_center) annihilate
the exact Nyquist mode and are far less stiff than the continuum estimate, the
effective omega_max here is ~100/Omega_i, so even dt = 0.02 is within the RK4
limit and nBSubcycle = 1 is stable (verified: the measured whistler frequency
is identical for nBSubcycle in {1,4,8,16,32}). 8 is chosen as a conservative
margin that is 4x cheaper than the previous 32 while still exercising the
sub-cycling machinery.
#BSUBCYCLE
8                       nBSubcycle

Single kinetic ion species (q = m = 1); the electron is an implicit
neutralizing massless fluid, so no electron particle species is present.
#PLASMA
1                       nSpecies
1.0                     mass
1.0                     charge

#UNIFORMSTATE
5.0                     rho [amu/cc]   Species 0: ions (n = 5 /cc)
0.0                     ux [km/s]
0.0                     uy [km/s]
0.0                     uz [km/s]
10000.0                 T [K]
1.0439e-8               bx [T]       uniform guide field Bx0 (B_code ~ 1)
0.0                     by [T]
0.0                     bz [T]
0.0                     ex [V/m]
0.0                     ey [V/m]
0.0                     ez [V/m]

Right-hand (whistler) circular polarization: B_z carries the opposite sign to
B_y so the transverse field rotates y -> z about +B0 (the electron-gyration
sense) for the wave travelling along +B0.
#TESTCASE
HybridWave

#WAVEIC
T                       rightHand      right-hand (whistler) helicity
1                       waveMode       box-fundamental (n=1) mode

#TIMESTEPPING
T                       useFixedDt
0.02                    dt

#NORMALIZATION
1.0e5                   lNormSI   [m]  ~ d_i so d_i ~ 1 in code units
1.0e5                   uNormSI   [m/s] tNorm = 1 s; sets cLight_SI = uNormSI

#MONITOR
50                      dnReport

#SAVELOG
10                      dnSavePic
10                      dnSavePT

#PARTICLES
2000                    nParticle per cell in X
1                       nParticle per cell in Y
1                       nParticle per cell in Z

#SAVEPLOT
1                       nPlot
z=0 fluid ascii pic     plotString
10                      dn
-1                      dt
1                       dx

#STOP
-1                      MaxIter
24.0                    TimeMax   ~6 whistler periods (n=1 mode, T = 3.9 s);
                                  this used to be 10 s because the grid-scale
                                  instability of the Hall advance swamped the
                                  signal after ~10 s - with the hyper-resistivity
                                  above the mode is stable for the whole run

#RUN
